export default "## Why Timing Matters\n\nPeriod bloating can follow a cycle pattern. It can also overlap with gas, constipation, loose stool, food changes, medicine changes, stress, illness, or pelvic pain.\n\nUse timing as a clue, not a diagnosis. Write down when bloating starts, when it peaks, and when it eases.\n\nIf gas or stool changes are the main issue, use the [period symptom tracker](/free/premenstrual-symptom-tracker) to log them.\n\nThis content is for notes and education. It is not a substitute for professional medical advice, diagnosis, or treatment.\n\n## Prompt-care check\n\nAsk for care promptly if bloating comes with:\n\n- Severe or worsening belly or pelvic pain.\n- Fever.\n- Vomiting.\n- Blood in stool.\n- Black stool.\n- Rectal bleeding.\n- Weight loss without trying.\n- Pregnancy or possible pregnancy.\n- Faintness or dizziness.\n- Symptoms that feel unsafe.\n\nDo not wait to finish a log if you feel very sick.\n\n## The Cycle Pattern\n\nSome people notice bloating in the [luteal phase](/resources/symptom-guides/luteal-phase-symptoms), the days after ovulation and before a period.\n\nOthers notice it on bleeding days, with cramps, loose stool, constipation, or gas.\n\nThe pattern matters more than a single day. If bloating is new, strong, or different from your usual pattern, write that down.\n\n## What to Track and When\n\n**Daily bloating severity.** Use a simple scale (none, mild, moderate, severe) and log it at the same time each day, ideally evening when bloating tends to peak. Consistency in timing matters more than precision in rating.\n\n**Onset and resolution relative to cycle day.** Mark when bloating first appears and when it eases. After a few cycles, you may see whether the pattern repeats.\n\n**Associated digestive symptoms.** Note constipation, loose stools, gas, and nausea alongside bloating.\n\n**Food and fluid intake.** You do not need a full food diary. Just note if something unusual happened on days when bloating was notably better or worse.\n\n## Patterns That Signal Something Else\n\nBloating can have more than one cause. Consider discussing your notes with a clinician if:\n\n- Bloating happens through most of the cycle.\n- Bloating is getting worse over time.\n- Severe bloating comes with pelvic pain or [cramps](/resources/symptom-guides/cramps-before-period).\n- Bloating comes with constipation, loose stool, blood in stool, fever, vomiting, or weight loss.\n- Bloating changes school, work, sleep, sex, or care tasks.\n\nTracking cannot diagnose the cause. It can help you bring a clear story.\n\nIf bloating comes with one-sided pelvic pain or pressure, the [ovarian cyst symptom checklist](/free/ovarian-cyst-tracking-kit) can help you bring a short, non-diagnostic note set to a clinician.\n\n## When to Talk to Your Provider\n\nBring your notes to a provider if bloating affects daily life, does not follow your usual pattern, comes with pain or stool changes, or worries you.\n\nA short log with cycle day, stool changes, pain, and red flags can help your provider decide what to check.\n\n[Floriva](/resources/best/best-private-period-tracker-apps) can keep cycle notes on your device. Paper works too. For privacy help, use the [digestive cycle data privacy checklist](/resources/privacy-in-practice/protect-your-symptom-and-condition-data).";
